= Solution
{parent=i}

**False.** A ternary atomic predicate gives a counterexample:
$$
\boxed{\phi=P_0^{(3)}(x_0,x_0,x_0).}
$$
This string contains one opening parenthesis and two commas. Thus its number of opening parentheses is smaller than its number of commas. A high-arity argument list contributes several commas while requiring only one pair of parentheses.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**False.** Choose a variable $x_j$ different from $x_i$ and take
$$
\boxed{\phi=\forall x_j\,P_0^{(1)}(x_j).}
$$
The variable $x_i$ does not occur at all, so in particular it is not free. Yet the formula has one universal-quantifier symbol and zero occurrences of $x_i$. The hypothesis on free occurrences of one variable does not control quantifiers binding other variables.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**False.** Let $\phi$ be $x_i\dot=x_i$ and let $t=x_j$ with $j\ne i$. There is no quantifier that could capture a variable, so $t$ is free for $x_i$ in $\phi$. <Capture-avoiding substitution> gives
$$
\boxed{\phi[t/x_i]=x_j\dot=x_j.}
$$
The number of occurrences of $x_i$ falls from two to zero, contradicting the proposed strict increase. Even <capture-avoiding substitution> of $x_i$ itself would leave the formula unchanged and refute the strict inequality.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**True.** In the formation rules, commas occur only between arguments in a function application or an atomic predicate application. Each such argument list includes an opening parenthesis. Subsequent construction of larger terms or formulas retains those symbols.

Equivalently prove by simultaneous <structural induction> on terms and formulas that an expression containing no opening parenthesis contains no comma. Variables and constants have neither; a comma-bearing function or predicate application already has an opening parenthesis; equality, connectives, and quantifiers cannot introduce a comma outside their constituent expressions. Taking the contrapositive proves the claim.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A collection of <logical connectives> is adequate, or has <functional completeness>, if every <Boolean function> of finitely many <propositional variables> can be expressed by a formula using those connectives and variables, with the usual convention for nullary truth constants stated if those are included.

An adequate example is $\boxed{\{\neg,\to\}}$. An inadequate example is $\boxed{\{\to\}}$: formulas using <logical implication> alone preserve truth when every input is true, so cannot express <negation>. These examples concern truth functions, rather than mere syntactic formation of formulas.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use <structural induction> on formulas. Every term denotes the unique element. Every atomic predicate is true by hypothesis, and every equality is true because its two terms denote that same element.

If both immediate subformulas of an <logical implication> are true, their <logical implication> is true. A universal or <existential quantification> ranges over the singleton domain; its matrix is true for the only possible value by induction, so either quantified formula is true. These are all the formation cases in the reduced language. Hence
$$
\boxed{\text{Every formula of the reduced language is true under every assignment in this structure.}}
$$

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**No.** The full language can express $\neg(x_0\dot=x_0)$, which is false in the singleton structure from part (i). Every formula of the reduced language is true in that structure under every <assignment in a first-order structure>. Therefore no formula of the reduced language can be logically equivalent to this negated equality.

This is a <singleton truth-preservation obstruction to expressive completeness>. Adding both quantifiers to <logical implication> does not repair the failure: on this particular nonempty singleton model, quantification cannot create a false formula from true <atomic formulas>.

