= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $f=\alpha\to(\beta\to\gamma)$ and $h=\alpha\to\beta$. Temporarily assume $f$, $h$, and $\alpha$. <Modus ponens rule> first gives $\beta\to\gamma$ from $f$ and $\alpha$, then $\beta$ from $h$ and $\alpha$, and finally $\gamma$.

Apply the <deduction theorem for propositional logic> successively to discharge $\alpha$, then $h$, then $f$. The result is
$$
\boxed{(\alpha\to(\beta\to\gamma))\to((\alpha\to\beta)\to(\alpha\to\gamma)),}
$$
the required axiom scheme. This derivation uses only assumptions, <modus ponens rule>, and the added deduction rule; no <negation> axiom is involved.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**The requested derivation does not follow from the printed axiom set.** Use a two-valued matrix interpretation with ordinary material <logical implication> and designated value $1$, but interpret the unary symbol $\neg$ by the constant function $N(0)=N(1)=0$.

Every instance of the first axiom $\alpha\to(\beta\to\alpha)$ is valid. In the replacement axiom, both $N(\beta)\to\alpha$ and $N(\alpha)\to\beta$ have value $1$, so its whole <logical implication> is valid as well. <Modus ponens rule> preserves the designated value. The deduction rule is sound for semantic consequence under this interpretation because <logical implication> is the ordinary truth-functional <logical implication>. Induction on derivations, allowing arbitrary sets of assumptions, therefore makes every theorem valid in this matrix.

But the old contraposition axiom has value
$$
(N(\beta)\to N(\alpha))\to(\alpha\to\beta)
=1\to(\alpha\to\beta),
$$
which is false when $\alpha=1$ and $\beta=0$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\text{The printed replacement system does not derive the old third axiom.}}
$$
This is a <truth-matrix countermodel for a Hilbert axiom set>. A nonstandard interpretation of a primitive symbol is legitimate for proving syntactic nonderivability: only preservation by the displayed axioms and rules is required. The source would need a stronger <negation> axiom or an extra <negation> principle to support its requested conclusion.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The old system does derive the replacement axiom. Here is a syntactic proof using only its axioms, <modus ponens rule>, and the permitted <deduction theorem for propositional logic>.

First derive explosion. From $U$ and $\neg U$, the first axiom gives $\neg V\to\neg U$. The old third axiom then gives $U\to V$, and <modus ponens rule> with $U$ gives any desired $V$.

Next derive the classical reductio rule. Suppose $\Gamma,\neg B$ derives both $C$ and $\neg C$. Let $T$ be the theorem $B\to B$, obtainable by applying the <deduction theorem for propositional logic> to the identity derivation from assumption $B$. Explosion under $\Gamma,\neg B$ gives $\neg T$, so the <deduction theorem for propositional logic> gives $\Gamma\vdash\neg B\to\neg T$. The old third axiom with $\alpha=T$, $\beta=B$ yields $\Gamma\vdash T\to B$. Since $T$ is a theorem, $\Gamma\vdash B$. Thus a contradiction under $\neg B$ permits deriving $B$ with that temporary assumption discharged.

Now assume $h=\neg\beta\to\alpha$ and $\neg\alpha$, and temporarily also assume $\neg\beta$. <Modus ponens rule> gives $\alpha$, contradicting $\neg\alpha$. The just-proved reductio rule discharges $\neg\beta$ and gives $\beta$. Finally discharge $\neg\alpha$ and $h$ by the <deduction theorem for propositional logic>:
$$
\boxed{(\neg\beta\to\alpha)\to(\neg\alpha\to\beta).}
$$
No propositional completeness theorem is needed in this derivation.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Only one <logical implication> of the printed equivalence is justified:
$$
\boxed{\Gamma\vdash_{L'_0}\phi\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\Gamma\vdash_{L_0}\phi.}
$$
The new system's first axiom is an old axiom, its replacement axiom is derivable in the old system by part (iii), <modus ponens rule> is shared, and its deduction rule is admissible in the old system by the theorem allowed in the source. Induction on a new-system derivation therefore translates it into an old-system derivation with the same assumptions.

The reverse <logical implication> is false for the displayed systems. Take $\Gamma=\varnothing$ and let $\phi$ be an instance of the old contraposition axiom, for example with distinct <propositional variables> in place of $\alpha,\beta$. It is an old-system theorem, but the matrix countermodel in part (ii) refutes its derivability in the new system. Thus the requested two-way equivalence cannot be concluded from these printed axioms.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $U=\forall x_i\neg\phi$ and $H=\neg U\to\psi$. All derivations below use the printed replacement axiom, so they do not rely on the false equivalence from part (a).

The identity <logical implication> $\neg A\to\neg A$ follows by the deduction rule. Substituting $\alpha=\neg A$, $\beta=A$ into the replacement axiom and applying <modus ponens rule> therefore gives double-negation elimination:
$$
\vdash\neg\neg A\to A.
$$
Universal instantiation gives $\vdash U\to\neg\phi$. Combine it with $\vdash\neg\neg U\to U$ using <modus ponens rule> under a temporary assumption and then the deduction rule, obtaining $\vdash\neg\neg U\to\neg\phi$.

In the replacement axiom now substitute $\alpha=\neg\phi$ and $\beta=\neg U$. Its antecedent is precisely the last theorem, and its consequent is $\neg\neg\phi\to\neg U$. Thus
$$
\vdash\neg\neg\phi\to\neg\forall x_i\neg\phi.
$$
Under the fixed assumption $H$ and temporary assumption $\neg\neg\phi$, <modus ponens rule> gives $\neg U$ and then $\psi$. Discharge the temporary assumption to obtain $H\vdash\neg\neg\phi\to\psi$.

Finally apply universal generalization. The variable $x_i$ is not free in $H$, because it is bound in $U$ and is not free in $\psi$ by hypothesis. Hence the side condition is satisfied and
$$
\boxed{\{\neg\forall x_i\neg\phi\to\psi\}\vdash\forall x_i(\neg\neg\phi\to\psi).}
$$
Generalization is applied after the temporary assumption has been discharged; applying it while that assumption remained open would not be justified.

