{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For nonzero $\alpha$, consider the exponents $\eta$ with $\omega^\eta\leq\alpha$. The set is nonempty because it contains zero, and bounded because $\omega^\eta\geq\eta$ by <transfinite induction>. Let $\delta$ be its supremum. If this supremum is a successor it is attained; if it is a limit, continuity of <ordinal exponentiation> gives $\omega^\delta\leq\alpha$. The zero case is immediate. Thus $\delta$ itself belongs to the set and is its maximum. Maximality gives
$$
\boxed{\omega^\delta\leq\alpha<\omega^{\delta+1}.}
$$
Put $\rho=\omega^\delta>0$. Since $\omega^{\delta+1}=\rho\cdot\omega=\sup_{N<\omega}\rho N$, there is a least positive <natural number> $N$ with $\alpha<\rho N$. We have $N\geq2$ because $\rho\leq\alpha$. Set $n=N-1$. Then
$$
\rho n\leq\alpha<\rho(n+1).
$$
For <ordinals> $\xi\leq\alpha$, the final segment of $\alpha$ after its initial segment $\xi$ has an order type $\zeta$ satisfying $\alpha=\xi+\zeta$. Apply this with $\xi=\rho n$. If $\zeta\geq\rho$, monotonicity would give $\alpha\geq\rho n+\rho=\rho(n+1)$, a contradiction. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\alpha=\omega^\delta n+\zeta,\qquad0<n<\omega,\qquad\zeta<\omega^\delta.}
$$
These intervals also make the leading exponent and coefficient unique. The remainder is unique because addition by a fixed left prefix is strictly increasing in its right operand.

