= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $\kappa=|\alpha|$ be the infinite <initial ordinal> of the given <ordinal>. A successor adds only one point, so $|\alpha+1|=\kappa$: one can absorb the additional point by shifting the initial copy of $\omega$ and fixing the rest.

The successor construction from part (a) partitions the new range into the old range, a new row of size $|\alpha+1|$, and a new column of size $|\alpha|$. By hypothesis each part has <cardinality> at most $\kappa$, and the old range already has <cardinality> $\kappa$.

A finite <disjoint union> of copies of a well-orderable infinite cardinal has that same <cardinality> without invoking the square theorem being proved. Explicitly, write each $\xi<\kappa$ uniquely as $\omega\eta+n$ with $n<\omega$. Interleave three copies by
$$
(\xi,j)\longmapsto\omega\eta+3n+j,\qquad j<3.
$$
This stays below $\kappa$ because an infinite <initial ordinal> is a limit <ordinal> and is closed under finite successor steps below it. It is <injective> by uniqueness of the quotient and finite remainder. The reverse <injection> is immediate, so the <Cantor-Schröder-Bernstein theorem> proves the finite-union claim.

Applying that bound to the three range pieces yields
$$
\boxed{|\gamma_{\alpha+1}|=\kappa=|\alpha+1|.}
$$
Only the well-orderability of <ordinals>, not a choice principle for arbitrary families of sets, was used.

